@galak 
Passing the status from the DMA callback is desired because `dma_get_status()` provides status of a the DMA peripheral that might have changed since the interrupt fired.

Let's say we are receiving data into a RX buffer that gets full but the status shows a write index at the beginning of the RX buffer like at index `1` for example (data wrapped around). As we keep track of the last write index into the DMA buffer between interrupts, we can correctly use the data from the buffer but because the UART API expects a continuous chunk of memory (see API definition below), we then need to call the UART callback **twice** ([see `uart_callback_t`](https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/5d9c65fe7f817e67cce7649874f429db9b6abf3c/include/zephyr/drivers/uart.h#L325)), instead of once, because the write index made progress, even though we are not done receiving UART data (idle interrupt didn't fire).

```c
/**
 * @brief UART RX event data.
 *
 * The data represented by the event is stored in rx.buf[rx.offset] to
 * rx.buf[rx.offset+rx.len].  That is, the length is relative to the offset.
 */
struct uart_event_rx {
	/** @brief Pointer to current buffer. */
	uint8_t *buf;
	/** @brief Currently received data offset in bytes. */
	size_t offset;
	/** @brief Number of new bytes received. */
	size_t len;
};
``` 

Here is a piece of logs from my running example. My buffer is 2048-byte long, and the message is 688-byte long.
The first line will lead to 2 calls to the UART callback: one for data from index `1729` to `2048` and one from `0` to `1`, instead of 1 call if we use information from the DMA module that the buffer is full.
```
2023-02-01 11:18:15.373 [00:03:35.665,000] <dbg> uart_stm32: async_evt_rx_rdy: rx_rdy: (1729 1)
2023-02-01 11:18:15.381 [00:03:35.668,000] <dbg> uart_stm32: async_evt_rx_rdy: rx_rdy: (1 369)
```

Because we know the interrupt reason from the DMA module, I thought it would be more efficient to directly use this piece of information and pass it to the DMA user, in this case the UART module.

I made a running example this morning that doesn't involve DMA API modifications and can probably open a new PR with these changes. EDIT: see #54306

Even though, I think this API change would allow for more flexibility regarding the usage of the DMA peripherals depending on the platform. There are reasons to have several interrupt sources so hiding them to the DMA user increases complexity for correct handling of these interrupts. The positive integers used as a status are platform-specific and not shared across implementations so it doesn't bring much complexity as they are defined in the platform scope, and not mandatory to use. Now this is my opinion, so I'll let you guys decide what's best between the 2 proposed solutions 🙂

